    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Supreme Council of Azerbaijan adopted a Declaration of Independence on 18 October 1991, affirmed by a referendum in December, days before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> ceased to exist on 26 December.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-nagorno-karabakh-war">First Nagorno-Karabakh War</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> in control of 14–16 percent of Azerbaijani territory by 1994; the Second War of 2020 and the offensive of September 2023 returned it all and ended with the expulsion of nearly all ethnic Armenians from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh">Nagorno-Karabakh</a>.</li><li>Oil and gas from the Azeri–Chirag–Guneshli and Shah Deniz fields, carried west by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku-tbilisi-ceyhan-pipeline">Baku–Tbilisi–Ceyhan pipeline</a> from 2006, raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a> twenty-fold between 1995 and 2012 while the New Azerbaijan Party kept power from 1993.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Nearly half of all the mud volcanoes on Earth are concentrated in Azerbaijan, and at Yanar Dag on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absheron-peninsula">Absheron Peninsula</a> a natural gas fire blazes continuously on a hillside, which is why <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> is known as the 'land of fire'.</p></aside>
    </section>

