    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty.jpg" alt="Qajar dynasty" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar dynasty</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Persia_(endonym;_Iran)_in_the_1860s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alexander Fadeyev (Faddeyev), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> was ruled first by Caucasian Albania and then by a succession of Persian empires. It remained part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> until the Russo-Persian wars of 1804–1813 and 1826–1828, when the treaties of Gulistan and Turkmenchay ceded the khanates north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Aras River</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. An Azerbaijani national identity emerged in Russian-held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>, Ganja and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a> at the end of the nineteenth century, and when the empire collapsed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musavat">Musavat</a> party declared the Azerbaijan Democratic Republic in May 1918, the first modern parliamentary republic in the Muslim world. It lasted 23 months: the Bolshevik 11th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> invaded in April 1920, as many as 20,000 Azerbaijani soldiers died resisting, and the Azerbaijan Soviet Socialist Republic followed.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> Baku supplied 80 percent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>'s oil on the Eastern Front. Under glasnost the dispute over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh">Nagorno-Karabakh</a>, an autonomous region with an Armenian majority, grew into open conflict and calls for independence, culminating in the Black January events in Baku. In 1990 the Supreme Council dropped the words 'Soviet Socialist', adopted a Declaration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">Sovereignty</a> and restored the flag of the Democratic Republic; after the failed Soviet coup of 1991 it adopted a Declaration of Independence on 18 October, affirmed by referendum in December, and the Soviet Union ceased to exist on 26 December.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Two-thirds of Azerbaijan is rich in oil and natural gas, an industry reaching back to antiquity. In September 1994 the State Oil Company of Azerbaijan Republic (SOCAR) signed a 30-year contract with 13 oil companies, among them Amoco, BP, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exxonmobil">ExxonMobil</a>, Lukoil and Equinor, opening deepwater fields untouched by Soviet exploitation; the State Oil Fund was set up to hold the revenue for future generations. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku-tbilisi-ceyhan-pipeline">Baku–Tbilisi–Ceyhan pipeline</a>, 1,774 km through Azerbaijan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, opened in May 2006 and can carry 50 million tons of crude a year; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">South Caucasus</a> Pipeline followed at the end of 2006, and the Trans-Anatolian and Trans Adriatic pipelines deliver Shah Deniz gas to Turkey and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p>The boom brought growth and strain alike. Chronic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> was tamed in the early 2000s and a new manat launched on 1 January 2006, but demand pushed inflation to 16.6 percent in the first quarter of 2007, and the economy shows signs of '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> disease'. By 2012 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a> had increased twenty-fold from its 1995 level. About 54.9 percent of the land is agricultural, growing grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, tobacco, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a> and sugar beet, with wine, livestock and a Caspian sturgeon fishery; tourism, damaged by the war of the 1990s, recovered in the 2000s. In March 2022 the population was 10,164,464, 52.9 percent urban; by the 2019 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> 94.8 percent were Azerbaijanis, and emigration restrained growth.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/first-nagorno-karabakh-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-nagorno-karabakh-war.jpg" alt="First Nagorno-Karabakh War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/first-nagorno-karabakh-war">First Nagorno-Karabakh War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karabakhwar01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nicholas Babaian, Oleg Litvin, Jalpeyrie, MarshallBagramyan, collage: Gulustan, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Armed Forces of the Republic of Azerbaijan were created by the law of 9 October 1991, though Army Day is kept on 26 June, when the Azerbaijan Democratic Republic founded its National Army in 1918. The new state was at once at war. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-nagorno-karabakh-war">First Nagorno-Karabakh War</a> with the region's Armenian majority, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, lasted until 1994; both sides committed atrocities and pogroms, among them the Khojaly massacre, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> pogrom, the Maraga massacre and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganja-azerbaijan">Kirovabad</a> pogrom. An estimated 30,000 people were killed and more than a million displaced, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> were left controlling 14–16 percent of Azerbaijani territory. Defeat fed coups at home: Colonel Surat Huseynov's insurrection of 1993, his second attempt in 1994 and the 1995 revolt of the OMON commander Rovshan Javadov.</p><p>By 2021 the armed forces had 126,000 active personnel and 17,000 paramilitary troops, a defence budget of $2.24 billion in 2020, 5.4 percent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a>, and an arms industry of its own; Azerbaijan cooperates with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> through the Partnership for Peace and sent peacekeepers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. On 27 September 2020 fighting resumed along the Line of Contact, and the six-week Second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh">Nagorno-Karabakh</a> War ended in a ceasefire that returned the seven districts and parts of Nagorno-Karabakh. In September 2023 an offensive ended the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artsakh-historic-province">Artsakh</a>, dissolved on 1 January 2024, and nearly all ethnic Armenians were expelled from the region.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/heydar-aliyev"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heydar-aliyev.jpg" alt="Heydar Aliyev" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/heydar-aliyev">Heydar Aliyev</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heydar_Aliyev_(crop).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Press Service of the President of the Republic of Azerbaijan, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The constitution adopted on 12 November 1995 describes a presidential republic with three branches of power. The president is elected by direct vote for a seven-year term, forms the Cabinet of Ministers and may veto but not dissolve the legislature. The unicameral Milli Majlis has 125 deputies elected by majority vote for five years. The president nominates the judges of the Constitutional, Supreme and Economic Courts and appoints the governors of the 66 rayons and 11 cities, grouped in 14 economic regions, so that local executive authority is a continuation of the presidency. Under article 48 the state is secular, though every religious community must register to meet, and registration is often denied.</p><p>In practice the government has functioned as an authoritarian regime. The New Azerbaijan Party founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heydar-aliyev">Heydar Aliyev</a> has ruled since 1993; Aliyev was re-elected in 1998, and on his death in 2003 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilham-aliyev">Ilham Aliyev</a> became party chairman and president, winning a third term in 2013 and in April 2018 a fourth in an election the main opposition boycotted as fraudulent. Freedom House rated the country 'not free' at 7 of 100 in 2024. The media environment deteriorated sharply in 2014, all foreign broadcasts are banned, journalists of Abzas Media, Toplum TV and Meydan TV were tried in 2024 and 2025, and in 2025 there were 375 political prisoners.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Azerbaijan is one of six independent Turkic states and an active member of the Organization of Turkic States and TÜRKSOY, and it has placed a particular emphasis on its 'special relationship' with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. It holds diplomatic relations with 182 countries and belongs to 38 international organizations, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-europe">Council of Europe</a> since 2001, the OSCE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>'s Partnership for Peace; it was a founder of GUAM, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-independent-states">Commonwealth of Independent States</a> and the OPCW. Its weight abroad rests on energy: the Southern Gas Corridor from Shah Deniz to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> is meant to reduce the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>'s dependence on Russian gas.</p><p>The wars reshaped the region's peoples. Many Russians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> fled in the 1990s, where the 1970 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> had counted 510,000 Russians and 484,000 Armenians, and the Armenian population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh">Nagorno-Karabakh</a> was expelled in 2023. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> has sought another image through spectacle: Ell and Nikki won the Eurovision Song Contest in 2011 and the city hosted it in 2012, then the 2015 European Games, the first Formula One Grand Prix in 2016, the Islamic Solidarity Games in 2017 and UEFA Euro 2020, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> has recognised mugham, ashiq art and the Azerbaijani carpet. Its revenues also bought influence: the Azerbaijani laundromat bribed foreign politicians and journalists to legitimise its elections, a practice termed caviar diplomacy.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/sari-gelin">Sari Gelin<span>date unknown</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ilham-aliyev"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ilham-aliyev.jpg" alt="Ilham Aliyev" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ilham-aliyev">Ilham Aliyev</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ilham_Aliyev,_December_2025_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Press Service of the President of the Republic of Azerbaijan, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The three decades since independence have been shaped by one family's rule and one region's war. Since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heydar-aliyev">Heydar Aliyev</a> took power in 1993 the Aliyev family and the New Azerbaijan Party have governed without interruption, criticised for election fraud, high economic inequality and domestic corruption even as the Azeri–Chirag–Guneshli and Shah Deniz fields transformed the economy. Amnesty International reported in 2015 the near wiping out of civil society and persistent reports of torture; Azerbaijan had the largest number of imprisoned journalists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> that year. The country ranks 81st on the Human Development Index and uses more biocapacity than it contains.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh">Nagorno-Karabakh</a> conflict, which cost some 30,000 lives and displaced over a million in the 1990s, turned decisively in Azerbaijan's favour in the six-week war of 2020 and in the offensive of September 2023, which dissolved the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artsakh-historic-province">Artsakh</a> on 1 January 2024 and expelled nearly all its ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>. On 8 August 2025 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilham-aliyev">Ilham Aliyev</a> and the Armenian prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikol-pashinyan">Nikol Pashinyan</a> signed a joint declaration at the White House committing to a peace deal, facilitated by the Trump administration, to end nearly four decades of conflict. The state now faces the tasks it has set itself, lessening dependence on oil through tourism and technology, while its political prisoners, banned broadcasters and 'not free' rating remain the measure of its rule.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Azerbaijan, independent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> since 1991, turned Caspian oil and gas into pipelines to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and a twenty-fold rise in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a>, fought three wars for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh">Nagorno-Karabakh</a> that ended in 2023 with the region's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> expelled, and has been ruled since 1993 by Heydar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilham-aliyev">Ilham Aliyev</a>'s New Azerbaijan Party amid charges of fraud and repression.</em></p>

